{
  "skillId": "simplify-ratios-fractions-decimals",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "A **fully simplified** ratio contains no fractions and no decimals. Clear them first — multiply every part by a power of $10$ for decimals, or by the lowest common denominator for fractions — and then divide by the HCF as usual.",
    "facts": [
      "Decimals: multiply by $10$ for one decimal place, $100$ for two — use the part with the **most** decimal places, e.g. $0.6:2.7=6:27=2:9$.",
      "Fractions: rewrite with the lowest common denominator, then multiply every part by that denominator, e.g. $\\frac{3}{4}:\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{9}{12}:\\frac{8}{12}=9:8$.",
      "Multiplying every part by the same number gives an equivalent ratio, so clearing the fractions or decimals does not change the comparison.",
      "If a ratio mixes forms — a decimal and a fraction, or a mixed number — write every part in one form first."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite any fractions with their lowest common denominator.",
      "Multiply every part by a power of $10$ (decimals) or by the common denominator (fractions).",
      "Divide every part by their HCF."
    ]
  },
  "practice": {
